WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.860
Suppose I tore a DNA molecule down the middle and threw one half away.

2
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.160
Could you rebuild the half I threw away, letter for letter?

3
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.000
By the end of this film, you can.

4
00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.730
Start with one building block, a nucleotide.

5
00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:24.830
It has three parts: a sugar called deoxyribose, a phosphate group, and a base.

6
00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.670
The base carries the information.

7
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.970
DNA uses four bases: adenine, thymine, cytosine and guanine.

8
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.030
We write them A, T, C and G.

9
00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:40.853
Nucleotides join into a chain called a strand.

10
00:00:41.953 --> 00:00:45.753
The sugar of one nucleotide bonds to the phosphate of the next.

11
00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:51.213
That is a covalent bond, a strong bond in which atoms share electrons.

12
00:00:52.613 --> 00:00:54.253
The bases stick out to one side.

13
00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:58.627
Now a second strand lines up, bases facing bases.

14
00:01:00.047 --> 00:01:04.227
Weak attractions called hydrogen bonds hold each facing pair together.

15
00:01:05.487 --> 00:01:07.327
But only two kinds of pair fit well.

16
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:11.827
A fits with T. C fits with G.

17
00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:18.457
Watch what happens when A meets C. Their edges do not match, so they fit badly.

18
00:01:19.657 --> 00:01:21.117
That is the base-pairing rule.

19
00:01:22.497 --> 00:01:25.577
A always pairs with T, and C always pairs with G.

20
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.320
Notice the two arrowheads.

21
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.420
Each strand has a direction, and the two strands point opposite ways.

22
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.260
Finally the ladder twists along its length into a double helix.

23
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.640
The backbones are the sides, and the base pairs are the rungs.

24
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:45.573
Here is one strand on its own.

25
00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:52.973
It reads G, A, T, T, C, A. Before I fill in its partner, write the partner strand

26
00:01:52.973 --> 00:01:53.973
yourself.

27
00:01:59.867 --> 00:02:08.007
Across from G goes C. Across from A goes T. So the partner reads C, T, A, A, G, T.

28
00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:10.647
Not one letter was a choice.

29
00:02:11.907 --> 00:02:14.227
One strand fixed every letter of the other.

30
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.580
So here is how a cell copies DNA.

31
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.220
Enzymes unzip the helix, and each old strand becomes a template.

32
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.820
A template is a pattern that sets the order of a new strand.

33
00:02:28.933 --> 00:02:33.733
New nucleotides line up on each template, each one matching the base it faces.

34
00:02:35.153 --> 00:02:37.393
When copying ends, there are two molecules.

35
00:02:38.873 --> 00:02:42.193
Each has one old grey strand and one new gold strand.

36
00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:45.973
Both carry the same base order as the original.

37
00:02:47.117 --> 00:02:49.357
So pairing tells the cell what goes where.

38
00:02:50.577 --> 00:02:55.657
Because A pairs only with T, and C only with G, each strand fixes its partner.

39
00:02:57.037 --> 00:03:01.097
Pairing does not yet explain how the cell gets almost every letter right.
